From: DEREK@alcor.org
Date:  Tue, 20 Sep 94 12:15:29 
Subject: [#94-9-318] In praise of the List

Hi folks.

In a recent private email exchange with Robin Hanson, I expressed 
my gratitude to him for his postings on extropians, and explained a 
bit about why I value the list itself.  He suggested that I might post 
some of my comments to the entire list, as a sort of morale booster.  
So, for those who are interested, here is part of what I wrote to 
Robin, in somewhat edited form. . .

***********
BEGIN INCLUDED SECTION

Perhaps we can attribute this [my preference for certain authors on 
the list such as Robin] to the particulars of my education level.  I'm 
conversant on many of the list topics on what you might call a "lay-
extropian" level, but I'm not very schooled in the advanced 
sciences.  The authors I value the most are the ones who seem to 
me to bring me into the ballpark of having a clue what is being 
talked about the fastest.  This requires both the ability to think 
about the really advanced abstract stuff in a rigorous manner, and 
the ability to write about it clearly enough that it can be parsed even 
by those who aren't able to contribute to the discussion.

This is what I love about the list the most, when it is as busy and 
enthusiastic as it is right now.  Those of you who are really on top 
of it get to interact with others on your level who can spur you into 
more focused and improved thinking, and those of us who aren't on 
your level (but who have enough background and good enough 
working brains to think well when we are prodded) get to lurk and 
learn.

In an early Foresight Institute update, Drexler published a little 
piece about how to study fields outside of one's own area of 
expertise.  In it, he advocates just diving right in, and not worrying 
when you don't understand everything.  In this manner, you can 
gain an idea as to which topics are most important in the field, 
which issues are significant, what obstacles are confounding the 
experts.  Then, when you're ready to be more rigorous, you have a 
much better focus for more directed learning.  It's a kind of top 
down approach to education.

That's what the list is for me, the top down portion of my 
education.  I'm studying other things in school (besides working at 
Alcor, I'm currently taking an EMT course, and next semester I'm 
starting chemistry, anatomy and physiology, and microbiology) but 
I read the list when I just want to let my mind wander in the realm 
of issues far beyond my current education.  It's very stimulating.

Anyway, that's the long version of why I like your stuff so much.  
You really do a great job of giving me the gestalt view of most of 
the topics you address, and that allows me to have a clue as to what 
is being talked about by both you and others.

END INCLUDED SECTION
*********

Naturally, there are other authors whose posts I value for similar 
reasons, but to name them all might slight some whom I would not 
like to offend.

In general, having lurked here on and off for more than a year, I 
would say that the quality of all discussions on whole has risen 
constantly over that time.  One of the best aspects of the ongoing 
dialogue is simply that it is almost always *civil.*  Indeed, an 
undercurrent of mutual respect and fascination with each other 
pervades most of what I see written.  As I'm sure you're all aware, 
this is a very rare thing.

I can't imagine a single forum in which to improve one's thinking 
faster.  Congratulations to all of us.

Forward in all directions!

Derek Ryan
derek@alcor.org

