From: hanson@hss.caltech.edu (Robin Hanson)
Date: Tue, 20 Sep 1994 15:10:18 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-320] Penrose's "Shadows of the Mind"

I just spent the last half hour reading Penrose's new book "Shadows of
the Mind" in the Caltech bookstore (I'm not willing to shell out $25
for it.)  This book is basically a much-revised version of his prior
book "The Emporer's New Mind".  And given that he's a very smart
world-renound tenured prof with lots of time to devote, and since he
got a huge amount of high-quality criticism on his last book, this
book is just much better, at least for arguing for his conclusion.  It
may not be as good a tutorial as his last book though.

Now I still think he's quite wrong, but this book will require a lot
more care to refute -- I wrote a critique of his first book for
Foresight Update, and I can see that level of discussion just wouldn't
do for this.

So where does he go wrong?  Mainly he keeps piling on unlikely
possibilities until his total scenario is quite unlikely, though still
possible in principle.  The only reason for entertaining such a
scenario he offers is his Godelian proof that math folk "understand"
math in a way computers couldn't possibly.  I didn't get to reading
his new proof, but I doubt if he can make it fly.  I didn't find
anyone familiar with Godelian math who though his last proof flew.

Expect the sparks to keep flying on this stuff for some time to come.

I did think his intro section where he laid out views A,B,C,D on AI
was well done - and illuminated issues I've seen clouded here.  I'd
have to call myself a B, and I thought his critique of A was worth
reading. 

Robin Hanson

