From: doug@OpenMind.com (Doug Cutrell)
Date: Tue, 20 Sep 1994 15:36:02 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-321] Penrose's "Shadows of the Mind"

Robin Hanson writes:

>given that he's a very smart
>world-renound tenured prof with lots of time to devote, and since he
>got a huge amount of high-quality criticism on his last book, this
>book is just much better, at least for arguing for his conclusion.  It
>may not be as good a tutorial as his last book though.

I agree with Robin Hanson's view regarding Penrose's overall argument (for
concluding that consciouness is "non-computable" in some sense).  However,
this new book contains the most in depth and comprehensive coverage I have
seen yet of the possible mechanisms for quantum mechanics in neurobiology,
and for the possibility of non-computability in quantum gravity.  I would
recommend the book to anyone interested in either of those two topics
(although the discussion is not of a detailed technical nature).  What he
says there does match up with what I have gathered from various sources
elsewhere (such as Michael Lockwood, David Deutsch, and Karl Pribram).

I think it is unfortunate that the general ideas of QM specific roles in
neurobiology, and of non-computability in physics, have been cast into an
association with Penrose's insistence that consciousness be non-computable.
These issues are completely orthogonal, and deserve to be considered
seriously on their own.

Doug

