From: sasha1@netcom.com (Alexander Chislenko)
Date: Tue, 20 Sep 1994 15:51:39 -0700
Subject: [#94-9-323] TERM: "acracy", "acrat", "acratic"

  I agree with Dave Krieger that 'anarchy' in the sense we use this word
is different from the well-known (and unpopular) concept of anarchy as
absense of order (which is what it means etymologically).

  This is not the meaning we want to convey.

  'Acracy' seems to indicate absense of [any] governance; it sounds a bit
better, though I would argue that
 
  1) governance doesn't necessarily indicate the presense of a State
      or coersion  
  2) in application to social systems, there is hardly any difference
     between governance and order.

 I would like to see a term that would mean absense of coersion in general,
by any social structure that may try to influence/restrict my life against
my will.

I am equally unhappy about being coersed by a territorial entity (State),
ideological organization (Church, Party), mafia, or any other social group.
So State is not the point here.

Probably, the term we are looking for should have a familiar form of 'A...CY'
where '...' is to be replaced by some euphonic synonym of 'coersion' or 'force'
from one of the traditional sources of etymo-borrowings.

If not the silly [i.e. semantically irrelevant]  linguo/phonic considerations,
ACOERSY would suit me just fine.

  Sasha.


