By interesting coincidence I just returned from a lecture Penrose gave here
at BU on exactly this topic.  I've read _The Emperor's New Mind_ (mostly...)
and I must say he presented himself much better in person, at least IMHO.
His meta-theory on QM seems rather reasonable to me (to paraphrase: `we
should consider that the answer to the dilemmas of current (computable)
theories of quantum phenomena may lie in a theory based on non-computability').
I'm not a physicist, but he was well received by the many that filled the
room.

However, once he started discussing the brain my skepticism grew.  He
suggested that the way the quantum effects affect brain function is through a
conformational change in the tubulin molecule.  This molecule is the building
block of microtubules in cells, which construct the cytoskeleton and mediate
transportation of molecules throughout the cell.  In neurons this has
significant effects on neural morphology and synaptic efficacy.  Furthermore,
the conformational change in microtubules is dependent upon the placement of an
electron, which may be subject to quantum effects.  Sounds plausible.  (I seem
to recall that this has been discussed here...).  However, the problem with the
theory is that it still offers no functional motivation for the evolution of
such a mechanism.  What non-computational problems did primitive organisms have
to solve in order to survive?  (Penrose does suggest that these mechanisms were
as central in our ancestors as they are in us - an easier position to defend
than one which might claim that they were a recent innovation)

The theory thus rests on two pieces of wishful thinking: 1) that the problem of
behavior is inherently non-computable, and 2) that quantum coherence implements
non-computable "computation".  The first of these is very questionable to me -
survival at its simplest merely required autocatalysis, followed some time by
gradient following methods (tumble and drift like paramecia), followed perhaps
by rudimentary conditioning...  Ok, not easy, but is it non-computable?
Consider that organisms are not interested in proofs but rather in good-enough
guesses.  And what examples of non-computable problems do we have that are not
seeking formal solutions?

This brings me to the other of Penrose's sources of support - the
non-computability of mathematical thought.  Here he invokes Godel's proof,
of relevance only to formal systems.  Being one of those who has rejected
the concept of thought as formal symbol manipulation, I likewise reject the
relevance of Godel's theorem to mental function.  And as far as mathematical
insight - is it really more than mere trial-and-error?  When I myself look at a
tiling problem I attack it visually, trying out various configurations until
something fits (what did Escher do?).  But perhaps I'm overapplying Penrose's
analogy...

>
>I think it is unfortunate that the general ideas of QM specific roles in
>neurobiology, and of non-computability in physics, have been cast into an
>association with Penrose's insistence that consciousness be non-computable.
>These issues are completely orthogonal, and deserve to be considered
>seriously on their own.
>

True.  The problem probably is that Penrose's theory is the most well known
outside physics circles.  Those of us outside tend to generalize them together.
But again, I question any theory that assigns a role to quantum mechanisms
working in the brain without providing either an evolutionary motivation for
them in simple behavior or an explanation how they may have developed through
phylogeny.

